IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ma-nouns`
IMPORT `ma-seat`

-- Worked cases for ma-seat.l4: at least one per question, with the facts a person in the seat would state.
-- Every expected value is written from the text of the Act before the run (see ma-tests-*.l4 for the rules
-- themselves).  Argument order is the order of the GIVEN of the question in ma-seat.l4.
--
-- Mei Ling bought a small bakery from Dev.  Before she signed, Dev told her the bakery took $20,000 a month; it took
-- $8,000.  Dev was the other party to the contract.  The cases below ask the questions she would ask.

§ `Cases -- s 1: can I rescind?`

-- Case 1.1.  Mei signed after Dev's statement; the statement is written into the contract; the contract has been performed;
-- and, leaving those aside, she could rescind for the misrepresentation without alleging fraud.  s 1: she is so entitled.
#ASSERT `Am I entitled to rescind despite the term or the performance` Yes Yes Yes Yes EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 1.2.  Only the statement became a term.
#ASSERT `Am I entitled to rescind despite the term or the performance` Yes Yes No Yes EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 1.3.  Only the performance.
#ASSERT `Am I entitled to rescind despite the term or the performance` Yes No Yes Yes EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 1.4.  Neither the statement became a term nor the contract performed: the "where" condition fails.
#ASSERT `Am I entitled to rescind despite the term or the performance` Yes No No Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 1.5.  She did not sign after any statement: the "where" condition fails.
#ASSERT `Am I entitled to rescind despite the term or the performance` No Yes Yes Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 1.6.  Otherwise she could not rescind without alleging fraud: the "if" condition fails.
#ASSERT `Am I entitled to rescind despite the term or the performance` Yes Yes Yes No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 1.7 (not covered).  She is not sure whether she could otherwise rescind: the Act does not answer on these facts.
#ASSERT `Am I entitled to rescind despite the term or the performance` Yes Yes Yes `I am not sure` EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Cases -- s 2: damages`

-- Case 2.1 (s 2(1)).  Dev, the other party, made the statement; Mei lost money; Dev would have been liable if he had lied;
-- he has proved neither reasonable ground nor belief.  Dev is liable although the statement was not fraudulent.
#ASSERT `Is the other party liable to pay me damages` Yes Yes Yes No No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 2.2.  Dev has proved both matters ("unless he proves"): the liability is lifted.
#ASSERT `Is the other party liable to pay me damages` Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 2.3.  Dev proved reasonable ground but not belief up to the time of the contract: the proviso is not met.
#ASSERT `Is the other party liable to pay me damages` Yes Yes Yes Yes No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 2.4.  Mei suffered no loss: the "where" condition fails.
#ASSERT `Is the other party liable to pay me damages` Yes No Yes No No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 2.5.  The statement was made by someone who is not a party to the contract: the "where" condition fails.
#ASSERT `Is the other party liable to pay me damages` No Yes Yes No No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 2.6 (not covered).  Mei does not know whether Dev would have been liable had he lied.
#ASSERT `Is the other party liable to pay me damages` Yes Yes `I am not sure` No No EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case 2.7 (not covered).  It is not yet known whether Dev has proved belief.
#ASSERT `Is the other party liable to pay me damages` Yes Yes Yes Yes `I am not sure` EQUALS NOTHING

-- Case 2.8 (the proviso alone).  Both matters proved: the proviso is met.
#ASSERT `Does section 2(1) give the other party a defence` Yes Yes EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 2.9.  Reasonable ground not proved: this section gives no defence on these facts.
#ASSERT `Does section 2(1) give the other party a defence` No Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 2.10.  Belief not proved.
#ASSERT `Does section 2(1) give the other party a defence` Yes No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 2.11 (not covered).  Not sure whether belief was proved, the other matter proved.
#ASSERT `Does section 2(1) give the other party a defence` Yes `I am not sure` EQUALS NOTHING

-- Case 2.12 (s 2(2)).  Mei claims rescission in court proceedings; the court thinks it equitable to keep the contract and award
-- damages, having regard to all three matters; the statement was not fraudulent.  The court may.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitrator award damages instead of rescission` Yes Yes Yes Yes `The court` Yes Yes Yes Yes EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 2.13.  An arbitrator decides.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitrator award damages instead of rescission` Yes Yes Yes Yes `An arbitrator` Yes Yes Yes Yes EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 2.14.  Mei has not claimed rescission in any proceedings: the "if" condition fails.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitrator award damages instead of rescission` Yes Yes Yes No `The court` Yes Yes Yes Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 2.15.  The court is not of the opinion that it would be equitable: the "if" condition fails.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitrator award damages instead of rescission` Yes Yes Yes Yes `The court` No Yes Yes Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 2.16.  Mei would not be entitled to rescind: the "where" condition fails.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitrator award damages instead of rescission` Yes Yes No Yes `The court` Yes Yes Yes Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 2.17.  Dev's statement was fraudulent: the "Where ... otherwise than fraudulently" limb fails, in the same "Where" clause as the entitlement to rescind.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitrator award damages instead of rescission` Yes No Yes Yes `The court` Yes Yes Yes Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 2.17a (not covered).  Mei is not sure whether Dev's statement was fraudulent.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitrator award damages instead of rescission` Yes `I am not sure` Yes Yes `The court` Yes Yes Yes Yes EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case 2.18 (not covered).  The claim is before a body that is neither the court nor an arbitrator.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitrator award damages instead of rescission` Yes Yes Yes Yes `Another body` Yes Yes Yes Yes EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case 2.19 (not covered).  The court did not have regard to the loss rescission would cause Dev.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitrator award damages instead of rescission` Yes Yes Yes Yes `The court` Yes Yes Yes No EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case 2.20 (not covered).  Mei is not sure she would be entitled to rescind.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitrator award damages instead of rescission` Yes Yes `I am not sure` Yes `The court` Yes Yes Yes Yes EQUALS NOTHING

-- Case 2.21 (s 2(3)).  Damages instead of rescission are available, and Dev is not liable under s 2(1): still available.
#ASSERT `Can damages be awarded instead of rescission whether or not liable under section 2(1)` Yes No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 2.22.  They are available and Dev is liable under s 2(1): still available.
#ASSERT `Can damages be awarded instead of rescission whether or not liable under section 2(1)` Yes Yes EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 2.23.  Not available under s 2(2): liability under s 2(1) makes no difference.
#ASSERT `Can damages be awarded instead of rescission whether or not liable under section 2(1)` No Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 2.24 (not covered).  Mei does not know whether s 2(2) damages are available.
#ASSERT `Can damages be awarded instead of rescission whether or not liable under section 2(1)` `I am not sure` No EQUALS NOTHING

-- Case 2.25 (s 2(3)).  Dev is liable under s 2(1) and an award has been made under s 2(2): it is taken into account.
#ASSERT `Must the award be taken into account` Yes Yes EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 2.26.  Dev is not liable under s 2(1): the "where" condition fails.
#ASSERT `Must the award be taken into account` No Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 2.27 (not covered).  Dev is liable but no award has been made: there is no award to take into account.
#ASSERT `Must the award be taken into account` Yes No EQUALS NOTHING

-- Case 2.28 (s 2(3), the amount).  Liable, an award of $5,000 made: the award is taken into account.
#ASSERT `How much of the award is to be taken into account` Yes Yes 5000 EQUALS JUST 5000
-- Case 2.29 (not covered).  Mei does not know the amount (0).
#ASSERT `How much of the award is to be taken into account` Yes Yes 0 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case 2.30 (not covered).  Dev is not liable under s 2(1): the rule does not apply, so no amount.
#ASSERT `How much of the award is to be taken into account` No Yes 5000 EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Cases -- s 3: does the other party's clause work?`

-- Case 3.1.  The contract says Dev is not liable for anything he said before it was signed.  The clause excludes a liability for a
-- misrepresentation made before the contract (a), restricts no remedy of Mei's (b), and does not satisfy the requirement of reasonableness.
#ASSERT `Is the clause of no effect` Yes No No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 3.2.  It satisfies the requirement of reasonableness: "except in so far as", so it is not of no effect.
#ASSERT `Is the clause of no effect` Yes No Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 3.3.  A clause that takes away Mei's remedy (b) and is not reasonable.
#ASSERT `Is the clause of no effect` No Yes No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 3.4.  A clause that concerns neither (a) nor (b): section 3 does not reach it.
#ASSERT `Is the clause of no effect` No No No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 3.5 (not covered).  Mei is not sure the clause reaches a statement made before the contract, and it is unreasonable.
#ASSERT `Is the clause of no effect` `I am not sure` No No EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case 3.6 (not covered).  The clause is within (a) but nobody can say whether it is reasonable.
#ASSERT `Is the clause of no effect` Yes No `I am not sure` EQUALS NOTHING

-- Case 3.6a.  The clause is within (a) and Dev, who relies on it, has not shown that it is reasonable: it is for him to show it, so it is of no effect.
#ASSERT `Is the clause of no effect given that the other party must show it is reasonable` Yes No No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 3.6b.  Dev has shown that it is reasonable: the exception is met, so it is not of no effect.
#ASSERT `Is the clause of no effect given that the other party must show it is reasonable` Yes No Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 3.6c (not covered).  Mei is not sure whether Dev has shown that it is reasonable.
#ASSERT `Is the clause of no effect given that the other party must show it is reasonable` Yes No `I am not sure` EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case 3.6d.  The clause concerns neither (a) nor (b): section 3 does not reach it, shown or not.
#ASSERT `Is the clause of no effect given that the other party must show it is reasonable` No No No EQUALS JUST FALSE

-- Case 3.7 (the burden).  Dev relies on the clause and says it is reasonable: it is for him to show it.
#ASSERT `Is it for the other party to show the clause is reasonable` Yes No Yes EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 3.8 (not covered).  The other party makes no such claim: the Act names only those claiming.
#ASSERT `Is it for the other party to show the clause is reasonable` Yes No No EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case 3.9.  Section 3 does not reach the clause: no burden arises.
#ASSERT `Is it for the other party to show the clause is reasonable` No No Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE

§ `Cases -- s 4: a statement or contract from long ago`

-- Case 4.1.  Mei's statement was made on 5 March 1990 and the contract of sale on 20 March 1990, before 12 November 1993; the Act was not
-- applicable under the Civil Law Act 1909: nothing in the Act applies.
#ASSERT `Does section 4 stop the Act applying` 1990 3 5 Yes 1990 3 20 No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 4.2.  The same, but the Act was applicable under section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909: section 4 does not bar it.
#ASSERT `Does section 4 stop the Act applying` 1990 3 5 Yes 1990 3 20 Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 4.3.  The day before: 11 November 1993.
#ASSERT `Does section 4 stop the Act applying` 1993 11 11 Yes 1993 11 11 No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 4.4.  The day itself: 12 November 1993 is not "before" it, so section 4 does not bar the Act.
#ASSERT `Does section 4 stop the Act applying` 1993 11 12 Yes 1993 11 12 No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 4.5.  Mei's own case: the statement and the contract in 2025, so section 4 does not bar the Act.
#ASSERT `Does section 4 stop the Act applying` 2025 6 1 Yes 2025 6 30 No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 4.6 (not covered).  Mei does not know the dates.
#ASSERT `Does section 4 stop the Act applying` 0 0 0 Yes 0 0 0 No EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case 4.7.  The statement date is not known but the contract of sale was made on 1 January 1990.
#ASSERT `Does section 4 stop the Act applying` 0 0 0 Yes 1990 1 1 No EQUALS JUST TRUE

§ `Cases -- the Act's words`

-- Case 5.1.  Mei's bakery is run through a company: the Interpretation Act says "person" and "party" include a company.
#ASSERT `Does the Act's word person or party include this party` `A company` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 5.2 (not covered).  Mei herself, an individual: the Interpretation Act names no individual.
#ASSERT `Does the Act's word person or party include this party` `An individual` EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case 5.3.  The claim is before the High Court of Singapore, a court of competent jurisdiction in Singapore.
#ASSERT `Is the body deciding my claim a court` `A court of competent jurisdiction in Singapore` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Case 5.4.  The claim is before a court that is not such a court.
#ASSERT `Is the body deciding my claim a court` `Any other court` EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Case 5.5.  The claim is before a mediator, who is no court.
#ASSERT `Is the body deciding my claim a court` `Any other body` EQUALS JUST FALSE
